\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Whitney and de Rham maps}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{The Whitney map #{\Wh} and the de Rham map #{\Rh} are the
two maps of \ref{fdip-00S2}. Dodziuk writes
#{W} and #{R} for them. The identity #{\Rh\Wh=\bbI} is his
\citet{(1.7), p. 84}{dodziuk1976hodge}.}

\p{His Laplacian on functions is the positive operator of
\citet{Section 5, p. 99}{dodziuk1976hodge}. For the inner
product in use, that operator is #{- \covsq}. His
codifferential is the adjoint #{\extdag} for the inner product
of his Definition 4.1.}

\p{The codifferential of
\citet{Definition 6.2, p. 15}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec},
on a #{(p+1)}-cochain, is their
#{\delta=(-1)^{np+1}\ast\extd\ast}. Here #{p} is the
degree of the value. Their definition writes that degree as
#{k}. Their #{\ast} is the discrete Hodge star from primal
cochains to dual cochains,
\citet{Definition 6.1, p. 15}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}.
It is not the dual of \ref{fdip-00QQ}, which writes
#{\star A=\tilde A\psI}. In these notes #{\delta} is only
the Kronecker delta, so their codifferential is not written
as #{\delta}. On functions,
the vertex formula of
\citet{pp. 23-24}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}
is a positive weighted sum of #{f} at the vertex minus #{f} at
the neighbors, under the positive volume convention. That
operator has the sign of #{- \covsq}. Before the vertex
formula, the source defines the smooth operator on functions
as their #{\delta\extd}.}
